+// Shorebird's self_hash and op_subgraph_hash, one pair per entry.
+//
+// These are the other two of SubgraphHasher's three Hashers -- "self" (its
+// vector at this+0xb0) and "object_pool" (this+0x78) -- and they take the
+// CLASSIFIED stream, where "subgraph" takes the words verbatim. What separates
+// them from each other:
+//
+//   * every push into "self" sits behind `cbnz w21`, so it receives entries
+//     only at depth 0. It is still NOT a function of the entry's own bytes,
+//     because the ordinal maps it reads are members of SubgraphHasher and are
+//     updated at every depth.
+//   * "object_pool" opens with a CodeName entry carrying
+//     Utils::StringHash(Code::Name()), pushed once by the constructor at
+//     0x100195f68 into this+0x78 -- and that is the only Hasher that gets one.
+//   * an external branch is where the two diverge structurally: "self" encodes
+//     it as ExternalBranchTargetIndex with a per-root ordinal (the try_emplace
+//     at 0x100196a28 is inside the depth-0 branch), while "object_pool"
+//     recurses and splices the callee's stream in at that point.
+//
+// 🔑 The pool ordinal COUNTS REPEATS. At 0x100196c28 the index is pushed onto
+// the walk-global vector at +0xe8 unconditionally, and only then does
+// try_emplace(index, that_vector.size() - 1) run against the map at +0x100
+// (0x100196c34-0x100196c50). So a first-seen index is numbered by how many pool
+// REFERENCES the walk has made, not how many distinct indices it has seen. The
+// field table at +0x180/+0x198 works the same way. The external-target map at
+// +0x128 does NOT -- it stores map.size(), which is distinct -- and the two
+// rules had to be read separately rather than assumed uniform.
+//
+// Measured on their own base.so: 1380 of 1380 self_hash and 1380 of 1380
+// op_subgraph_hash, as digests. Their self_hash takes 1170 distinct values over
+// 1380 functions and their op_subgraph_hash takes 1380, so for the latter the
+// truth partition is the discrete one and a partition comparison would have
+// been vacuous; digest equality is the criterion for both.
+struct ShorebirdClassifiedHashes {
